---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import DocsNav from '../components/DocsNav.astro';
import InstallServer from '../components/InstallServer.astro';

const groups = [
  {
    title: 'Getting Started',
    links: [
      { href: '#quickstart', label: 'Quick Start' },
      { href: '#requirements', label: 'Requirements' },
      { href: '#architecture', label: 'Architecture' },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Central Server',
    links: [
      { href: '#server-config', label: 'Database Config' },
      { href: '#server-env', label: 'Keys & Tokens' },
      { href: '#server-status', label: 'Status Pages' },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Reliability',
    links: [
      { href: '#heartbeat', label: 'Heartbeat Monitors' },
      { href: '#watchdog', label: 'Watchdog' },
    ],
  },
  {
    title: 'Agents',
    links: [
      { href: '#agent-install', label: 'Agent Registration' },
      { href: '#agent-config', label: 'Interval Tweaks' },
      { href: '#agent-trouble', label: 'Troubleshooting' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Layout title="Documentation | Blood Kings Monitoring" description="Official documentation for installing, configuring, and managing Blood Kings Monitoring server and remote probing agents.">
  <div class="docs-layout-container container">
    <DocsNav groups={groups} label="On this page" />

    <!-- Docs Content Area -->
    <div class="docs-main-content" data-copy="Copy" data-copied="Copied" data-copy-failed="Copy failed">
      <header class="docs-head">
        <h1>Documentation</h1>
        <p class="docs-intro">Install the server, add monitors and connect agents.</p>
      </header>
      <!-- Section 1: the one install guide (components/InstallServer.astro), the same as /download/#server. -->
      <section id="quickstart" class="docs-section">
        <span class="badge badge-accent">Developer Guide</span>
        <h2>Quick Start</h2>
        <p>This sets up the central server on ordinary PHP web hosting. It is the same guide as on the <a href="/download/#server">download page</a>.</p>
        <InstallServer lang="en" />

        <h3>Then: your first monitor</h3>
        <p>In the app, open <strong>Infrastructure → Add New Monitor</strong>. You can track websites (HTTP/HTTPS), TCP ports, Minecraft, TeamSpeak and Discord servers, servers and OpenWrt routers with an agent, or <a href="#heartbeat">heartbeats</a> from jobs that report in on their own. The legacy PHP admin (<code>admin.php</code>) is mostly Czech; the <code>/app</code> dashboard is in English and Czech.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2 -->
      <section id="requirements" class="docs-section">
        <hr class="docs-divider" />
        <h2>System Requirements</h2>
        <p>The central server is plain PHP and runs on ordinary shared web hosting; a VPS works too. What the hosting must offer is step 1 of the <a href="#quickstart">Quick Start</a>.</p>

        <h4>Central Server:</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Storage:</strong> the application plus a MySQL/MariaDB database that grows with your check history</li>
          <li><strong>Software:</strong> PHP 8.2+ (CI runs 8.4), Apache with <code>.htaccess</code> and <code>mod_rewrite</code>, and MySQL or MariaDB. The server runs without Docker; only the Python agent has a Docker option.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Remote Agent:</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>OS:</strong> Linux (any system with Bash, or Python 3), Windows 10/11/Server, OpenWrt/Turris OS, or Raspberry Pi OS</li>
          <li><strong>Network:</strong> Outgoing HTTPS access (port 443) to your central server; nothing inbound</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3 -->
      <section id="architecture" class="docs-section">
        <hr class="docs-divider" />
        <h2>Architecture Overview</h2>
        <p>Blood Kings combines active checks run by the server and reports that agents push to it.</p>

        <div class="architecture-diagram card">
          <div class="arch-block">
            <strong>Remote Agent</strong>
            <span>(Linux, OpenWrt, Windows, Docker)</span>
            <span class="arrow">↓ HTTPS POST (Metrics)</span>
          </div>
          <div class="arch-block highlight">
            <strong>Central Server</strong>
            <span>(PHP / MySQL or MariaDB)</span>
            <span class="arrow">↓ JSON API</span>
          </div>
          <div class="arch-block">
            <strong>Public Status Page</strong>
            <span>(the app at /app/public)</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p>
          <strong>Passive Telemetry (Agents):</strong> The agent is a script that cron (or the Task Scheduler) starts on the monitored machine: every minute on OpenWrt, every 5 minutes by default elsewhere. Each run reads system resources (CPU, memory, disks, network), reports them to the central server via secure POST requests. This means you do not have to expose open incoming firewall ports on the monitored hosts.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Active Probing (Pinger):</strong> The central server&#39;s cron run checks targets directly: HTTP/HTTPS requests (with an optional keyword and certificate expiry) and TCP connection checks. There is no ICMP ping and no UDP check; a &quot;ping&quot; is a TCP connect.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Central Server Sections. The connection itself is written once, in the Quick Start. -->
      <section id="server-config" class="docs-section">
        <hr class="docs-divider" />
        <h2>Central Server Database Config</h2>
        <p>The server connects to MySQL or MariaDB through PDO. The installer at <code>/app/setup</code> writes the connection into <code>status/config.php</code> (step 4 of the <a href="#quickstart">Quick Start</a>, which also shows the manual way). <code>config.sample.php</code> explains every other constant; the one most installs change is the time zone, which PHP and the database session then share:</p>
        <div class="docs-code-wrapper">
          <pre tabindex="0"><code>define('TIMEZONE', 'Europe/Prague');</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="server-env" class="docs-section">
        <hr class="docs-divider" />
        <h2>Keys & Tokens</h2>
        <p>Three optional keys. Each is read as a constant in <code>config.php</code> (in capitals), then as an environment variable, then from the app's <strong>Settings</strong>; the first one found wins.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>CRON_KEY</code>: the key <code>cron.php</code> asks for when it is called over HTTP (<code>?key=...</code>). While it is empty, anyone who knows the address can start a collection run; the command-line cron needs no key. The installer gives every new install its own.</li>
          <li><code>AGENT_REGISTRATION_TOKEN</code>: lets the Bash and OpenWrt agents register themselves (<code>--register</code>). While it is empty, the server accepts <code>CRON_KEY</code> in its place.</li>
          <li><code>METRICS_TOKEN</code>: the bearer token for the Prometheus endpoint <code>metrics.php</code>. While it is empty, the endpoint is off.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="docs-code-wrapper">
          <pre tabindex="0"><code>define('CRON_KEY', 'a-long-random-string');</code></pre>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="server-status" class="docs-section">
        <hr class="docs-divider" />
        <h2>Public Status Pages</h2>
        <p>Create status pages in the app under <strong>Status Pages</strong>: each has its own title, address (<code>/app/public?page=…</code>) and set of monitors, and can be hidden from the public. Visitors can subscribe to incident updates by e-mail there, and each page has an SVG badge to embed.</p>
      </section>
      <!-- Agent Sections -->
      <section id="heartbeat" class="docs-section">
        <hr class="docs-divider" />
        <h2>Heartbeat Monitors</h2>
        <p>Active checks only reach what is reachable over the network. A backup that runs at 3 AM and fails silently is invisible to them - there is nothing to ping. Heartbeat monitors invert the direction: the job reports in, and the monitor goes down when it stays silent.</p>
        <p>Create a monitor of type <strong>Heartbeat</strong>, set how often the job should report, then call the URL it gives you at the end of your job:</p>
        <div class="docs-code-wrapper">
          <pre><code>curl -fsS -m 10 "https://your-server/status/heartbeat.php?token=YOUR_TOKEN"</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p>If the job itself fails, say so explicitly - the monitor then goes down immediately instead of waiting out the interval:</p>
        <div class="docs-code-wrapper">
          <pre><code>curl -fsS -m 10 "https://your-server/status/heartbeat.php?token=YOUR_TOKEN&amp;status=fail&amp;msg=tar%20exited%202"</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p>A monitor that has never received a signal reports <strong>unknown</strong>, not down. It is not failing - nothing is known about it yet, and alerting on an outage that never happened is its own kind of lie.</p>
        <p>The token is the only thing authorising the endpoint. Keep it out of shared logs; if it leaks, anyone can report in on your behalf and the monitor will stay green while the backup is long dead.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="watchdog" class="docs-section">
        <hr class="docs-divider" />
        <h2>Watchdog: who watches the collector</h2>
        <p>Everything is collected by one cron job. If that job dies, the application does not crash - it keeps serving the last known states and looks perfectly healthy. Of all the ways monitoring can fail, this is the worst, because it never announces itself.</p>
        <p>The server therefore records every completed collection run and exposes it:</p>
        <div class="docs-code-wrapper">
          <pre><code>curl -s "https://your-server/status/api.php?action=collection_health"</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p>The response carries <code>stale: true</code> once the last run is older than the configured limit - and also when the collector has never run at all, because "no data" is not the same as "all good".</p>
        <p>A Cloudflare Worker checks that endpoint every five minutes. It runs outside the monitored host on purpose: a watchdog sharing a machine with the thing it watches dies with it. Point it at a Discord webhook once and it will speak up on its own:</p>
        <div class="docs-code-wrapper">
          <pre><code>cd apps/worker &amp;&amp; npx wrangler secret put WATCHDOG_DISCORD_WEBHOOK</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p>Without that secret the watchdog still checks, but it can only log - and it admits as much on <code>/api/watchdog</code>, rather than looking like it is guarding something.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="agent-install" class="docs-section">
        <hr class="docs-divider" />
        <h2>Agent Registration & Setup</h2>
        <p>Agents are self-contained scripts for Bash, Python, PowerShell and OpenWrt routers. The <a href="/download/#agents">download page</a> has the steps for every platform: the same ones the app prints when you add a monitor, there with your address and key filled in.</p>
        <p>For many machines, the Bash and OpenWrt agents can register themselves with the registration token (see <a href="#server-env">Keys & Tokens</a>). Both arguments are positional, and the second one is your server's <code>agent_api.php</code>:</p>
        <div class="docs-code-wrapper">
          <pre tabindex="0"><code>/opt/bk-agent/agent.sh --register YOUR_REGISTRATION_TOKEN https://YOUR-DOMAIN/status/agent_api.php</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p>Without the address the agent registers against its <code>localhost</code> default. It stores the key it receives in <code>agent.cfg</code> next to the script (<code>agent_openwrt.cfg</code> on a router). The Python and PowerShell agents have no register command: they take <code>API_URL</code> and the <code>AGENT_KEY</code> from the monitor's settings in <code>agent.cfg</code>. <a href="/download/#agent-register">More on registration</a>.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="agent-config" class="docs-section">
        <hr class="docs-divider" />
        <h2>Agent Intervals & Remote Actions</h2>
        <p>Agents run from cron: every 5 minutes on servers, every minute on OpenWrt routers. For OpenWrt routers, opt-in HMAC-signed Remote Actions can be enabled by setting <code>REMOTE_ACTIONS_ENABLED=1</code> in <code>agent_openwrt.cfg</code> next to the script.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="agent-trouble" class="docs-section">
        <hr class="docs-divider" />
        <h2>Agent Troubleshooting</h2>
        <p>If an agent stops reporting telemetry, verify outgoing HTTPS access to port 443, then run it by hand and read its log (it sits next to the script):</p>
        <div class="docs-code-wrapper">
          <pre tabindex="0"><code>/opt/bk-agent/agent.sh --verbose
tail -n 50 /opt/bk-agent/agent.log</code></pre>
        </div>
        <p>The <a href="/download/#agent-trouble">download page</a> has the same checks for OpenWrt, Windows and Docker.</p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>

  <script>
    import { addCopyButtons } from '../lib/copy-code';

    const docs = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.docs-main-content');
    if (docs) {
      const { copy = 'Copy', copied = 'Copied', copyFailed = 'Copy failed' } = docs.dataset;
      addCopyButtons(docs, '.docs-code-wrapper', { copy, copied, failed: copyFailed });
    }
  </script>
</Layout>

<style>
  .docs-layout-container {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 260px minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 4rem;
    padding-top: 4rem;
    padding-bottom: 6rem;
  }

  /* Content styles */
  .docs-main-content {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 4rem;
  }

  .docs-head h1 {
    font-size: var(--text-4xl);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .docs-intro {
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
  }

  .docs-section h2 {
    font-size: 2rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .docs-section h3 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .docs-section h4 {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .docs-section p {
    font-size: 1rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.2rem;
  }

  .docs-section ul {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
  }

  .docs-section li {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    color: var(--text-mid);
  }

  .docs-divider {
    border: 0;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--border);
    margin-bottom: 4rem;
  }

  /* Code wraps. The <pre> scrolls, not the wrapper: the copy button
     (lib/copy-code.ts) sits in the wrapper and must not scroll away with a
     long command. Dark in both themes, like every code block. */
  .docs-code-wrapper {
    position: relative;
    background: var(--surface-inset);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: 1.2rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    margin: 1rem 0;
  }

  /* Room for the copy button; this scoped padding would otherwise beat the
     layered .has-copy rule in global.css. */
  .docs-code-wrapper:global(.has-copy) {
    padding-top: 2.75rem;
  }

  .docs-code-wrapper pre {
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .docs-code-wrapper pre:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--brand-500);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .docs-code-wrapper code {
    color: var(--code-text);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
  }

  /* Diagram styles */
  .architecture-diagram {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    padding: 2rem;
    align-items: center;
    background: var(--surface-1);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .arch-block {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    background: var(--surface-2);
    padding: 1rem 2rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    text-align: center;
    width: 280px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  .arch-block strong {
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: var(--text-hi);
  }

  .arch-block span {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--text-low);
  }

  .arch-block.highlight {
    border-color: var(--brand-500);
    background: rgba(176,0,32,0.02);
  }

  .arrow {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 1rem;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--brand-500);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  /* Below 992 px DocsNav swaps the sidebar for an "On this page" menu. */
  @media (max-width: 992px) {
    .docs-layout-container {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 2rem;
    }
  }
</style>
